I see that while I was gone for the summer, people were feverishly making
changes to one of my favorite computer games ever.  I guess this is good,
but I have yet to get my hands on the new version.  But that's not
really the reason I am writing this note.
	You see, I don't mean to take up space on your board, but I know
that the people that read these notes are the ones that would be most
able to help me.  The truth is, plain and simple, that I am in the process
of designing a game much like our favorites (NetHack, Moria, Omega, etc.).
I am basing the game on the older editions of AD&D texts, and I'm trying
to implement as much as possible, but the advice of others is always a
blessing.
	Here are some of the things that I have included in the current
design:
	Parties of up to six(?) player characters.  These characters are
	able to move in two ways, party and individual movement.  Party
	movement is simply the movement of the entire party in a particular
	marching order, following the leading movements of the party leader.
	Individual movement allows certain characters to take advantage of
	certain class abilities (e.g. thief) and become a scout, spy,
	whatever.

	Parties of non-player characters moving through the same dungeon
	as the PC's, killing and looting along the way.  These characters
	move with some motive, and will have an alignment, as will the PC's.
	If the alignment of the encountered party if hostile, the attack
	will be immediate, etc.

	Creatures in the dungeon, depending on their intelligence level,
	may attack in formations, utilizing their footmen in the front
	while keeping their heavy duty clerics and witch-doctors in the back
	to cast offensive and defensive spells.

	All monsters specified in the AD&D Monster Manuals, as well as all
	character classes and races in the Player's Handbook.

	Torches, as well as lanterns and magic swords, illuminate in a certain
	radius, depending on the item, and will cast shadows if an object is
	blocking the path.  Here is an example:
				....
			      ......  <- Notice the char can't see here
			      .....###     where the torch does not illuminate
			       #.@...#
         The '@' has torch --->#.@...'g
			       #..@..#
			    ####.@.....
			       .......
			        ....

	Fire can be used as an offensive and defensive weapon.  PC's can
	throw flasks of oil at creatures and the like, but the fire will
	not just die out.  If the flask breaks on something flammable, the
	object itself will begin to burn.  This fire will spread.  So, a wise
	party confronted by a powerful creature(s), could always pour oil in
	a trail on the floor, leading around the room, and then light the oil.
	Since the fire will spread around it, and there is oil on the
	surrounding floor, the fire will quickly create a situation much like
	that in the bar in "Raiders of the Lost Ark."

	There are several important things about the dungeon.  Most of all,
	is that it is not created randomly.  A person, who would be
	the DM in standard AD&D, creates the dungeon and and setting using
	the brother to this program.  This person would specify the dungeon
	makeup, creatures, and objects.  There is an entire language just
	for the creation of the dungeon which utilizes such things as 
	an event list, in which events to occur in the future are stored,
	trap activation, and more.

	Oh yes, one more thing, intelligent creatures are able to use
	anything they possess...

These are only a few of the things to be included.  I hope you can now see
the scope of the project, and will help me if you can.  All I am asking
is that you, the experienced players of these games, offer some suggestions
as to what you would like to see in a game of this sort.  Should I include
religious pantheons?  Is weather simulation important in the game?  These
are things I ask myself, but no-one can answer all the questions alone.
Feel free to send me any ideas, (ANYTHING!), even if it may sound stupid,
it may lead to an important inclusion or deletion.
					Thanks for your patience,
